# File         : PARAMS.google
# Description  : Core/memory configuration for the Google core spec, expressed in Scarab
#                parameters. (Started as a copy of the Mockingjay (HPCA'22) config; the
#                front end, cache hierarchy and DRAM have since been respecified.)
#
#
# IMPORTANT Scarab-vs-matthew caveats:
#   * Cache-level naming is INVERTED in Scarab: icache/dcache = L1, mlc = L2, l1_* = LLC.
#   * FetchWidth and decode width share one knob (IC_ISSUE_WIDTH == DECODE_WIDTH), so
#     --decode_width 6 sets BOTH the fetch and decode bandwidth to 6.
#   * Scarab has no per-cache MSHR count. Requested MSHRs are APPROXIMATED:
#       L1D=16 -> --mem_req_buffer_entries 16 ; L2=32 -> --queue_mlc_size 32 ;
#       LLC=64 -> --queue_l1_size 64 ; L1I=8 has no separate knob (unmodeled).
#     Consider --hier_mshr_on 1 if MSHR fidelity matters: it makes queue_mlc_size /
#     queue_l1_size behave as hierarchical MSHR reservations rather than plain queues.
#   * Per-cache READ/WRITE/PREFETCH queue sizes (a ChampSim RQ/WQ/PQ structure) do NOT
#     exist in Scarab at ANY level and are therefore UNMODELED -- requested L1I 64/64/32
#     and L2 32/32/16 are both dropped. Scarab instead has one shared mem_req_buffer plus
#     per-stage queues (queue_mlc_size / queue_l1_size / queue_bus_out_size /
#     queue_core_fill_size), none of which split by request direction. The only
#     request-type differentiation available is PRIORITY (--mem_priority_*), not capacity.
#   * Cache inclusion is NON-INCLUSIVE (NINE) and not configurable: nothing back-invalidates
#     an inner level when an outer level evicts.
#   * NO TLB IS MODELED AT ALL -- this is an absent mechanism, not an approximation, and is
#     the largest fidelity gap in this file. Scarab has ZERO tlb parameters; the only TLB
#     code in the simulator is two power-model counters (POWER_ITLB_ACCESS in icache_stage.c,
#     POWER_DTLB_ACCESS in exec_stage.c). power_scarab_config.cc states it plainly: "Scarab:
#     models perfect tlb" with total_misses hard-coded to 0, and its 128-entry figure is a
#     literal in the McPAT XML emitter that has no effect on timing.
#     Consequences: address translation is FREE (no latency), NEVER misses, and generates NO
#     page-walk memory traffic. The requested ITLB/DTLB 64 entries / 4-way / 1-cycle / 8 MSHRs
#     are all unmodelable -- there is no structure to size, no latency to set, and nothing
#     that can miss. This matters for the datacenter workloads here: large-footprint server
#     applications are often TLB-bound, and real page walks would compete with demand traffic
#     for L2/LLC capacity, so a perfect TLB makes the cache hierarchy look cleaner than it is
#     and understates pressure on any replacement-policy study run on this config.
#     (--addr_translation below is a physical-address MAPPING scheme -- it affects cache
#     indexing and DRAM row locality -- but it is not a TLB and carries no cost model.)
#   * DRAM REFRESH INTERVAL IS NOT SETTABLE. ramulator.cc passes no tREFI/nREFI to ramulator,
#     so refresh comes entirely from the chosen --ramulator_speed grade. Every DDR3 grade in
#     ramulator's speed table uses nREFI = 7.8 us (e.g. 6240 cycles at 1.25 ns), i.e. a ~64 ms
#     refresh window. The requested 32 ms window (tREFI 3.9 us) would need a ramulator patch;
#     as configured the model refreshes HALF as often as the spec, so it slightly understates
#     refresh overhead.
#   * DRAM is DDR3. tCAS/tRCD/tRP/tRAS (6/6/6/13) are pinned by the spec and are CYCLE counts
#     (= 7.5/7.5/7.5/16.25 ns at tCK 1.25 ns). If the source spec quotes those in NANOSECONDS
#     instead -- ChampSim configs do -- the correct values are tCL/tRCD/tRP 5 and tRAS 10.
#     Secondary timings (tCWL/tBL/tCCD/tWTR) are taken from ramulator's own DDR3-1600 speed
#     table, not from the spec. Peak bandwidth is 1 ch x 8 B x 1600 MT/s = 12.8 GB/s.
#   * Core frequency (4 GHz) is assumed (not given in the core spec); it sets the ns<->cycle
#     bridge to DRAM. Change --chip_cycle_time to rescale.
#   * The RS/FU execution topology is NOT part of the spec; it is inherited from PARAMS.kmhv3
#     (known-good, aggressive) and is tunable. The branch predictor, BTB, RAS and DIB ARE
#     spec'd and set accordingly.
#   * Spec fields with NO Scarab equivalent, left unmodeled:
#       - ifetch_buffer_size 64 / decode_buffer_size 32 / dispatch_buffer_size 32. Scarab's
#         front end is FTQ (--fe_ftq_block_num) -> uop queue (--uop_queue_length) -> IDQ
#         (--idq_size), which is a different decomposition; no 1:1 mapping exists, so these
#         are left at their inherited values rather than guessed.
#       - scheduler_size 128. Scarab sizes reservation stations per port (--rs_sizes, wired to
#         --rs_connections and --fu_types). Current total 186 vs the spec's 128; collapsing to
#         one unified RS means rewriting all three knobs together.
#       - schedule_latency 0 / execute_latency 0. No Scarab knobs; these stages have no
#         separate configurable latency.
#       - mispredict_penalty 1. Scarab derives recovery cost structurally from the pipeline
#         rather than as a flat penalty. --extra_redirect_cycles / --extra_recovery_cycles are
#         the nearest additive knobs and are correctly left at 0.
#   * register_file_size 128 is a SINGLE unified file in the spec; Scarab splits integer and
#     vector. Both are set to 128, so the aggregate is 256 physical registers, not 128.
#   * branch_predictor "ctagescl" is mapped to Scarab's --bp_mech tagescl (TAGE_SC_L_CONFIG_64KB).
#     Confirm the spec's variant is the 64KB TAGE-SC-L; 'tagescl80' is the 80KB alternative.
#   * Prefetching is DISABLED per the spec (--pref_framework_on 0). Note several individual
#     pref_*_on flags default TRUE (including pref_ul1_on, the LLC trigger), but they are all
#     inert: pref_init() and every pref_common.c entry point return early on
#     !PREF_FRAMEWORK_ON, so nothing is allocated or trained.

## Simulation Parameters
--mode                          full
--model                         cmp
--sim_limit                     none
# num_cores 1. Stated explicitly because --private_l1 divides L1_SIZE and L1_BANKS by it.
--num_cores                     1
# heartbeat_frequency 10000000 (Scarab default is 1000000)
--heartbeat_interval            10000000
# page_size 2 MB huge pages (Scarab default is 4096). Feeds --addr_translation below, so it
# changes physical addressing and therefore cache indexing and DRAM row locality.
# init_memory asserts L1_LINE_SIZE <= VA_PAGE_SIZE_BYTES and
# LOG2(VA_PAGE_SIZE_BYTES) <= NUM_ADDR_NON_SIGN_EXTEND_BITS (21 <= 48 here); both hold.
--va_page_size_bytes            2097152

## Core Parameters

# Femtoseconds; 4.0 GHz assumed (Mockingjay core frequency unspecified). Used for energy
# and as the core<->DRAM cycle bridge.
--chip_cycle_time               250000

### Fetch Stage
--fetch_off_path_ops            1

# Decoupled frontend FTQ
--fe_ftq_block_num              64

#### ICache -- Mockingjay L1 I$: 32 KB, 8-way, 4-cycle
--icache_size                   32768
--icache_assoc                  8
--icache_line_size              64
--icache_latency                4
--icache_read_ports             2

### DIB (decoded instruction buffer) -- Scarab models this as the uop cache.
### 256 entries, 8-way => 32 sets x 8 ways. uop_cache_width (uops held per entry) is not
### part of the spec and is left at 8.
--uop_cache_enable              1
--uop_cache_width               4
--uop_cache_lines               256
--uop_cache_assoc               8
--iprf_on_uop_cache_hit         1

### Branch Predictor. Spec says "ctagescl"; currently set to tage64k (a CBP-submodule TAGE,
### NOT TAGE-SC-L). If the 64KB TAGE-SC-L is wanted instead, use --bp_mech tagescl
### (TAGE_SC_L_CONFIG_64KB in bp/tagescl.cc); 'tagescl80' is the 80KB variant.
--cfs_per_cycle                 6
--bp_update_at_retire           0
--update_bp_off_path            1
--bp_mech                       tage64k

### L0 (early) branch predictor DISABLED. The spec has a single branch_predictor and no early
### predictor -- but bp_mech_l0 defaults to BIMODAL_BP and bp_l0_latency to 1, so
### bp_l0_enabled() ((BP_MECH_L0 != NUM_BP) && (BP_L0_LATENCY > 0)) was silently TRUE and this
### config has been running a two-level predictor. bp_l0_latency 0 is the disable; there is no
### string for NUM_BP in bp_table.def, so bp_mech_l0 cannot be set to "none".
### bp.c:213 then requires BP_MAIN_LATENCY == 1 on the no-L0 path. That also keeps
### bp_btb_for_main()'s BTB_MAIN_LATENCY <= BP_MAIN_LATENCY test true at 1 <= 1, so the flat
### main BTB stays reachable. (With L0 ON the constraints are instead BP_MAIN_LATENCY > 1 AND
### BP_MAIN_LATENCY < DECODE_CYCLES -- unsatisfiable once decode_cycles is the spec's 1, which
### is exactly the assert that fired.)
--bp_l0_latency                 0
--bp_main_latency               1

#### BTB -- 8192 entries, 8-way, FLAT (no L0/L1 hierarchy), no access latency.
### Matches ChampSim's basic_btb, which is a single structure of sets=1024 x ways=8 = 8192
### entries with NO latency constant at all -- the BTB resolves in the same cycle as the
### prediction, and front-end cost comes from mispredict_penalty and the fetch pipeline.
### The L0/L1 levels below were kmhv3 inheritance, not part of this spec, so they are off.
### On btb_main_latency: bp.h's bp_btb_for_main() only tests BTB_MAIN_LATENCY <= BP_MAIN_LATENCY
### (3) to decide whether the main BTB is usable, and ft.cc only consults the value in the
### BTB_MAIN_LATENCY > BP_MAIN_LATENCY recovery path. So ANY value 0..3 is behaviourally
### identical here; 1 is set as the plain "available immediately" encoding.
--btb_mech                      generic
--btb_entries                   8192
--btb_assoc                     8
--btb_main_latency              1
# L0/L1 disabled -- the entries/assoc/latency lines below are inert, kept only so the
# hierarchy can be restored by flipping the two *_present flags back to 1.
--btb_l0_present                0
--btb_l0_entries                128
--btb_l0_assoc                  4
--btb_l0_latency                1
--btb_l1_present                0
--btb_l1_entries                6144
--btb_l1_assoc                  4
--btb_l1_latency                2
--btb_off_path_writes           1
--perfect_nt_btb                0

#### CRS (return address stack) -- 64 entries
--enable_crs                    1
--crs_entries                   64
--crs_realistic                 1

### iBP (ITTAGE)
--enable_ibp                    1
--ibtb_mech                     tc_tagged
--ibtb_off_path_writes          1
--tc_entries                    4096
--tc_assoc                      4

### Decode Stage
# fetch_width 6 / decode_width 6. IC_ISSUE_WIDTH == DECODE_WIDTH, so this one knob sets both.
--decode_width                  6
# decode_latency 1 (was 5, kmhv3 inheritance)
--decode_cycles                 1

### Instruction Decode Queue
# No clean mapping: ChampSim's ifetch_buffer_size 64 / decode_buffer_size 32 /
# dispatch_buffer_size 32 are three separate buffers, where Scarab's front end is
# FTQ (--fe_ftq_block_num) -> uop queue (--uop_queue_length) -> IDQ (--idq_size).
# Left at 64 rather than guessed; see the caveat block.
--idq_size                      64

### Map Stage
# dispatch_latency 1 (was 5, kmhv3 inheritance)
--map_cycles                    1
--reg_renaming_scheme               1
# register_file_size 128. ChampSim has ONE unified physical register file; Scarab splits
# integer and vector, so setting both to 128 gives 256 physical registers in aggregate
# rather than the spec's 128. There is no single-file mode -- see the caveat block.
--reg_table_integer_physical_size   128
--reg_table_vector_physical_size    128

### Issue Stage
# execute_width 4
--issue_width                   4

--rs_fill_width                 0
# RS / FU topology inherited from PARAMS.kmhv3 -- NOT changed to match the spec.
# ChampSim has one unified scheduler_size 128; Scarab has per-RS sizes wired to
# --rs_connections and --fu_types (a 10-port topology), so collapsing to a single 128-entry
# RS would mean rewriting all three together. Current total is 97+70+19 = 186 entries vs the
# spec's 128. --issue_width above still caps dispatch to 4/cycle regardless of port count.
--rs_sizes                      97 70 19
--rs_connections                b0100110011 b1000001100 b0011000000
--fu_types                      b00100100111111100111001001111110010010011111110011100100111111 b10010000110111100011111001110111001000011011110001111100111011 b00000001000000001000000010000010000000100000000100000001000001 b00000001000000001000000010000010000000100000000100000001000001 b00000000110111100011011001110110000000011011110001101100111011 b01001000000000000111001001111110100100000000000011100100111111 b00000010000000010000000100000010000001000000001000000010000001 b00000010000000010000000100000010000001000000001000000010000001 b00000000000000000011001001110110000000000000000001100100111011 b00000001000000001000000010000010000000100000000100000001000001

### Exec Stage

### Load/Store Queue -- lq_size 128, sq_size 72
--lsq_enable                    1
--load_queue_entry_num          128
--store_queue_entry_num         72

### DCache. Size/assoc/latency are NOT from the spec above (which gives no L1D block) --
### they are carried over and should be replaced when the L1D spec is available.
### Ports do come from the spec: lq_width 2 -> read ports, sq_width 2 -> write ports.
--dcache_size                   49152
--dcache_assoc                  12
--dcache_line_size              64
--dcache_cycles                 5
--dcache_read_ports             2
--dcache_write_ports            2
--dcache_banks                  8

### Reorder/Retire Stage
# RetireWidth = 5
--node_ret_width                5
# ROB = 352 entries
--node_table_size               352

--stores_do_not_block_window    1
--prefs_do_not_block_window     1


## Uncore  (Scarab naming: mlc = L2, l1_* = LLC)

### L2  (mlc) -- 2 MB, 16-way, 10-cycle, 32 MSHRs, non-inclusive.
### Non-inclusive needs no knob: Scarab never back-invalidates on an outer-level eviction
### (the only cache_invalidate in the memory system is on the prefetch cache), so the
### hierarchy is already NINE. The "if exclusive cache" comments in memory.c are stale --
### no exclusivity is implemented and there is no param for it.
### 32 MSHRs -> --queue_mlc_size 32 in the Memory section below.
### Read/write/prefetch queues (32/32/16) have NO Scarab equivalent -- see the caveat block.
--mlc_present                   1
--mlc_size                      2097152
--mlc_assoc                     16
--mlc_line_size                 64
--mlc_cycles                    10
--mlc_banks                     8

### LLC  (l1_*) -- 4 MB per core, private (1 per core), 16-way, 20-cycle, 64 MSHRs.
### CAREFUL: with --private_l1 1, init_memory gives each core L1_SIZE / NUM_CORES (and
### L1_BANKS / NUM_CORES), so --l1_size is the TOTAL, not the per-core size. At NUM_CORES 1
### these coincide; for an N-core run set --l1_size to N * 4194304 to keep 4 MB per core.
### Both L1_SIZE % NUM_CORES and L1_BANKS % NUM_CORES are asserted to be 0.
### 64 MSHRs -> --queue_l1_size 64 in the Memory section below.
--private_l1                    1
--l1_size                       4194304
--l1_assoc                      16
--l1_line_size                  64
--l1_cycles                     20
--l1_banks                      8
--l1_interleave_factor          64


### Prefetcher -- disabled to isolate the replacement policy (flip to 1 to enable)
--pref_framework_on             0
--pref_stream_on                0


### Memory
--addr_translation              random

--mem_priority_ifetch           0
--mem_priority_dfetch           1
--mem_priority_dstore           2
--mem_priority_iprf             3
--mem_priority_dprf             4
--mem_priority_wb               5
--mem_priority_wb_nodirty       5

# Per-cache MSHR approximation (Scarab has no explicit per-cache MSHR knob):
#   L1D = 16 outstanding
--mem_req_buffer_entries        16
#   L2  = 32 outstanding
--queue_mlc_size                32
#   LLC = 64 outstanding
--queue_l1_size                 64
--bus_width_in_bytes            8

### DRAM (ramulator): DDR3, 800 MHz I/O clock (= 1600 MT/s, DDR is 2x the clock),
### 1 channel x 1 rank x 8 banks, 64 GB, 6-6-6-13 tCAS/tRCD/tRP/tRAS.
### Bandwidth: 1 ch x 8 B x 1600 MT/s = 12.8 GB/s (~12.5 GB/s/core at 1 core).
###
### DDR3 rather than DDR4 because the spec's 8 flat banks and 6-6-6-13 string are DDR3:
### ramulator's DDR3 Level enum is {Channel, Rank, Bank, Row, Column} with NO bank-group
### level, and every DDR3 org has exactly 8 banks. --ramulator_bankgroups is therefore
### unused here and set to 1 so it does not read as meaningful.
###
### 64 GB = 8 banks x 524288 rows x 2048 cols x 8 B. Note capacity is not really a
### constraint: --ramulator_use_rest_of_addr_as_row_addr is "on", so the row index absorbs
### every remaining physical address bit and no two addresses ever alias regardless of the
### row count (Memory.h reports dram_capacity = max_address). What these numbers actually
### control is ROW SIZE -- cols x 8 B = 16 KB per row -- and hence row-buffer locality.
--ramulator_standard            DDR3
--ramulator_speed               DDR3_1600H
--ramulator_org                 DDR3_8Gb_x8
--ramulator_channels            1
--ramulator_ranks               1
# DDR3 has no bank groups; this value is ignored by the DDR3 model.
--ramulator_bankgroups          1
--ramulator_banks               8
--ramulator_chip_width          8
--ramulator_rows                524288
--ramulator_cols                2048
--ramulator_scheduling_policy   FRFCFS_Cap
--ramulator_readq_entries       64
--ramulator_writeq_entries      64
--ramulator_record_cmd_trace    FALSE
--ramulator_print_cmd_trace     FALSE
# tCK = 1.25 ns => 800 MHz I/O clock => 1600 MT/s
--ramulator_tCK                 1250000
# 6-6-6-13 tCAS/tRCD/tRP/tRAS, IN CYCLES (Scarab's ramulator timing params are cycle counts).
# At 1.25 ns/cycle that is 7.5 / 7.5 / 7.5 / 16.25 ns.
--ramulator_tCL                 6
--ramulator_tRCD                6
--ramulator_tRCDR               6
--ramulator_tRCDW               6
--ramulator_tRP                 6
--ramulator_tRPpb               6
--ramulator_tRPab               6
--ramulator_tRAS                13
# secondary timings from ramulator's own DDR3-1600 speed table (DDR3.h), not pinned by the
# spec. DDR3 has a single tCCD (4); the S/L split is a DDR4 bank-group concept, so both are
# set to the DDR3 value.
--ramulator_tCWL                8
--ramulator_tBL                 4
--ramulator_tCCD                4
--ramulator_tCCDS               4
--ramulator_tCCDL               4
--ramulator_tWTR                6
--ramulator_tWTRS               6
--ramulator_tWTRL               6
--dram_tech_in_nm               32

## Other

### Debug
--debug_inst_start              0
--debug_inst_stop              -1
--debug_cycle_start             0
--debug_cycle_stop             -1

## Stats and Params
--dump_params                   1
--dump_stats                    1
--dump_trace                    0

####################################
--set_off_path_confirmed        1
--order_beyond_bus              1
--mem_ooo_stores                1
--mem_obey_store_dep            1
